<ProseLayout
  title="About | Aragorn Wang"
  description="Background, education, and what I'm working on."
>
  <h1>about</h1>

  <p>
    I'm an undergraduate computer science student at the Colorado School of Mines, finishing a B.S.
    in Computer Science with a minor in Applied Mathematics and Statistics. Most of my time outside
    coursework goes to the ARIA Lab on campus, where I'm working on novel localization methods for
    mobile robots, building on semantic segmentation and object registration. That work took 1st
    place oral presentation at the 2026 MURF Symposium, and the first paper from it is in writing
    now.
  </p>

  <p>
    I came to Mines after a high-school stretch at STEM School Highlands Ranch, where I co-founded
    the Computer Science Honor Society and led software for FIRST Robotics Team 4418, then took
    college math classes at the University of Colorado Denver alongside high school. I got into ML
    through hardware-adjacent work. Two summers at Lockheed Martin Space (an AR app for full-scale
    3D model viewing in 2021, then ML research on satellite digital twins in 2022) put me at the
    intersection of machine learning and physical systems before I'd taken a college ML class. The
    pattern continued through Mines: SEAKR Engineering on satellite boot software in 2024, Ricoh USA
    on synthetic-data pipelines for industrial robotics in 2025, Northrop Grumman's AIR Lab on
    physics-informed ionosphere forecasting and computer vision for UAVs through late 2025. In
    retrospect, the same two questions kept showing up. How do you reduce a model's appetite for
    real-world data, whether through synthetic generation (Ricoh), physics-informed priors
    (Northrop), or geometric structure as an inductive bias for perception (ARIA Lab)? And how do
    you actually ship software that has to run inside hard deployment constraints, whether that's
    embedded boot software (SEAKR) or accelerator-constrained vision on UAVs (Northrop)? This past
    summer I was at Google as a SWE intern on the YouTube AI team, working on efficient neural
    architectures for video on custom accelerators, which is the deploy-side question at scale.
  </p>

  <p>
    The research direction I want to take into a PhD is generalist robotics: a single agent that
    takes a natural-language instruction and executes it on an unfamiliar platform in an unfamiliar
    environment. Vision-Language-Action models are the closest thing we have to that today, and the
    two open problems that interest me most are the two that surfaced in my past work. Data
    efficiency: how little real-world interaction does a generalist policy need before it
    generalizes off-distribution? Deploy-time efficiency: how do you take a multi-billion-parameter
    generalist and run it inside a robot's compute, latency, and power budget without losing the
    generalist behavior? Both sit between clean modeling abstractions and the constraints of real
    embodiment, and the groups attacking them most directly are working at the intersection of
    imitation learning, sim-to-real, and on-device inference. That's the kind of group I'd like to
    do my PhD in.
  </p>

